\chapter{The domain: a game of ropes for climbing knots}

Roppi is a browser game for learning the knots of sports climbing. A player
sees a rope drawn in three dimensions, grabs it with the mouse, and moves it.
The shapes the rope takes are the knots the player is learning to recognise
and tie. The player is a complete beginner.

\section{Purpose}

Roppi teaches climbing knots through hands-on manipulation, not memorised
diagrams. A knot is a shape the rope holds, and the shape changes only when
the rope is moved. Each knot is connected to its job in the climbing process:
why it is tied at that point, and what a mistake looks like.

\section{The problem}

Climbing knots are usually learned from diagrams and from an instructor's
hands. A diagram shows one picture of a knot and says nothing about how it is
built up or how it can go wrong. A beginner needs to see the rope move through
the crossings that make the knot, and to be told when the knot they have
built is the one they meant to build. Roppi makes the crossings visible and
checks the result.

``Solved'' means that a beginner can build each lesson's knot from nothing,
and the game names the knot the rope actually forms.

\section{Scope}

In scope:

\begin{itemize}
\item A rope rendered in 3D in the browser, moved with the mouse. Touch is not
required in the first version.
\item A rope model with minimal tension, so the rope feels responsive. It keeps
the rope from passing through itself or through an anchor.
\item Recognising a named knot by its topology: two shapes are the same knot
when one can be dragged into the other without passing through the rope.
\item Three teaching modes: guided, sandbox, and quiz.
\item The knots of sports climbing as a process. The priority areas are
rappel, anchoring, belaying and cleaning. Prusik and other friction hitches
are included for their shape only.
\end{itemize}

Deliberately out of scope:

\begin{itemize}
\item Load, holding and slipping behaviour. A knot that would hold or fail
under force is not simulated.
\item Engineering-accurate rope physics: strength, friction and wear.
\item Cutting, splicing and rope joins that change the rope's topology.
\item Fishing, sailing and other uses that are not climbing.
\item Multiplayer, accounts and online play.
\item Certification. Roppi is a proof of concept and a reference. Knots are
for learning the shape, and a learner must practise them with a qualified
expert. The product says so.
\end{itemize}

\section{Who it is for}

A complete beginner who wants to learn climbing knots before going on a wall,
and who will practise them with an expert.
